{#
  TESTIMONIALS, THE CONTENT LAYER — Figma 673:1638, cards 673:1650.

  THIS PARTIAL IS NO LONGER A SECTION EITHER. Its section, stage, photo, dim and
  runway are gone into partials/story.njk; what is left is the heading and the
  card row that rise over the shared band during beats B and C.

  THE ROW IS A MARQUEE, and 673:1638's nine card instances are what it is drawn
  from — three testimonials repeated three times, the designer showing a row that
  continues past both edges. It was built once as a snap-scroller centred on the
  node's rest state; this is the other reading of the same node, and the one
  asked for.

  SIX ITEMS, TWO IDENTICAL SETS, AND THE MACRO IS WHY. A seamless loop needs the
  second half to be pixel-identical to the first — so both come from one macro
  rather than two copies of the markup that could drift the first time a field
  changes. _testimonials.css animates the strip from -50% to 0, which moves the
  cards RIGHTWARD and lands set B exactly where set A began.

  THE SECOND SET IS FURNITURE, NOT CONTENT. aria-hidden on the item and
  tabindex="-1" on its link, so a screen reader reads three testimonials and Tab
  visits three cards — not six. The clone class is what CSS removes entirely in
  both static regimes.

  A LINK THAT DRIFTS UNDER THE CURSOR IS A LINK YOU CANNOT CLICK, so the strip
  freezes on hover and on focus-within. That is not decoration: it is what makes
  the three cards clickable at all.

  THE WHOLE CARD IS THE LINK, so everything inside it is phrasing content —
  spans, not headings and paragraphs. All three point at the same place: the
  recommendations tab of the LinkedIn profile, where the unabridged text lives.

  short/roleShort RATHER THAN quote/role, and the data file explains why: the
  node's card is 327 tall with a three-line quote and a one-line title, and
  neither the full recommendation nor Amir's four-clause role fits that. The
  archive fields stay complete; these are what the card prints.

  #testimonials IS NOT ON THIS BOX. It is a zero-height marker in the runway, at
  the scroll offset where the cards settle — see story.njk. An id on this layer
  would sit inside the sticky stage, so jumping to it would land the reader at
  the very start of the About sequence instead of on the cards.
#}

  <div class="testimonials__content">
    <h2 class="testimonials__heading">Testimonials</h2>

    {#- ONE MACRO, CALLED TWICE. `clone` is the only difference between the two
        sets: it adds the class CSS removes when the marquee is off, and it takes
        the copy out of the accessibility tree and out of the tab order. -#}
    {%- macro card(person, clone) %}
    <li class="testimonials__item{% if clone %} testimonials__item--clone{% endif %}"
        {%- if clone %} aria-hidden="true"{% endif %}>
      <a
        class="testimonial-card"
        href="{{ person.link }}"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        {%- if clone %} tabindex="-1"{% endif %}
      >
        <span class="testimonial-card__avatar">
          {%- if person.avatar %}
          <img
            class="testimonial-card__photo"
            src="{{ person.avatar }}"
            alt=""
            width="96"
            height="96"
            decoding="async"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          {%- else %}
          {#- NO HEADSHOT SUPPLIED. The initials are derived from `name` in the
              data file, so this can never disagree with the name printed below
              it. Setting `avatar` swaps it for a photo. -#}
          <span class="testimonial-card__initials" aria-hidden="true">{{ person.initials }}</span>
          {%- endif %}

          {#- THE QUOTE BADGE. 30px white disc at the avatar's lower right, and
              the mark inside it is TYPE, not artwork: 673:1654 is a flattened
              vector 12.923 x 9.261, and Figtree's own ” has an ink aspect of
              1.3948 against that box's 1.3954 — a 0.04% match, so the vector is
              this glyph. _testimonials.css carries the size and the two offsets
              that put its ink on the node's coordinates. -#}
          <span class="testimonial-card__badge" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </span>

        <span class="testimonial-card__quote">{{ person.short }}</span>
        <span class="testimonial-card__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="testimonial-card__name">{{ person.name }}</span>
        <span class="testimonial-card__role">{{ person.roleShort }}</span>
      </a>
    </li>
    {%- endmacro %}

    {#- THE VIEWPORT CLIPS, THE TRACK MOVES. They cannot be the same element: a
        box that clips its own overflow clips it in its own coordinate space, so
        an animated track would carry its window along with it and nothing would
        ever leave the frame. -#}
    <div class="testimonials__viewport">
      <ul class="testimonials__track">
        {%- for person in testimonials %}{{ card(person, false) }}{% endfor %}
        {%- for person in testimonials %}{{ card(person, true) }}{% endfor %}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
